1
00:00:01,368 --> 00:00:05,943
How can you two be so sure
that it's not an affair?

2
00:00:07,433 --> 00:00:11,943
<i>You were so suspicious of me and Mrs. Sonoda.</i>

3
00:00:18,761 --> 00:00:20,445
[Episode 7]
We met at the laundromat,

4
00:00:20,445 --> 00:00:23,813
and we just talked
until the laundry was done.

5
00:00:23,813 --> 00:00:25,985
I think it's the same thing.

6
00:00:27,209 --> 00:00:29,017
You're right.

7
00:00:39,873 --> 00:00:43,153
Sorry about your cheek. Here's some ice.

8
00:00:43,153 --> 00:00:44,997
Thanks.

9
00:00:47,605 --> 00:00:51,377
I'm sorry for taking up your time.

10
00:00:51,377 --> 00:00:53,151
I'm sorry.

11
00:00:54,269 --> 00:00:56,005
No problem.

12
00:01:00,233 --> 00:01:02,765
- Let me just confirm everything.
- All right.

13
00:01:02,765 --> 00:01:07,689
Your relationship with my wife was just
talking at the laundromat once a month.

14
00:01:07,689 --> 00:01:09,757
- Yes.
- You went to Nagano

15
00:01:09,757 --> 00:01:12,101
to see your estranged parents.

16
00:01:12,101 --> 00:01:15,621
- Yes.
- And you invited my wife to go along.

17
00:01:15,621 --> 00:01:17,465
Yes.

18
00:01:18,085 --> 00:01:21,997
At the service area,
you got off the bus alone.

19
00:01:21,997 --> 00:01:24,881
And then you disappeared without going home.

20
00:01:24,881 --> 00:01:26,633
Yes.

21
00:01:27,773 --> 00:01:30,589
Well, I'm curious about the reason, but...

22
00:01:30,589 --> 00:01:33,120
It's none of my business,
and you don't have to tell me.

23
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:36,229
- But still...
- I've left Sho with Mr. Miyauchi,

24
00:01:36,229 --> 00:01:38,145
so I shouldn't be too late.

25
00:01:38,933 --> 00:01:40,769
Right.

26
00:01:47,165 --> 00:01:49,173
Well, then...

27
00:01:49,173 --> 00:01:52,770
Can we talk on the way? Just the two of us.

28
00:01:54,497 --> 00:01:56,209
All right.

29
00:02:00,673 --> 00:02:02,521
See you later.

30
00:02:02,521 --> 00:02:04,341
All right.

31
00:02:15,913 --> 00:02:17,686
I'm sorry.

32
00:02:18,445 --> 00:02:21,445
What happened to your wife...
it was my fault.

33
00:02:21,445 --> 00:02:23,186
I'm sorry.

34
00:02:30,981 --> 00:02:32,761
I won't hit you.

35
00:02:32,761 --> 00:02:34,728
Even though you probably want me to.

36
00:02:35,461 --> 00:02:38,713
About the disappearance... it's a long story.

37
00:02:38,713 --> 00:02:41,913
No, it's fine. It really doesn't matter.

38
00:02:42,541 --> 00:02:44,345
Actually, I don't care.

39
00:02:44,345 --> 00:02:47,283
I don't care about anything except Azusa!

40
00:02:50,929 --> 00:02:55,978
I think there were things I lacked.

41
00:02:56,777 --> 00:02:59,186
So instead of talking to me at home,

42
00:02:59,869 --> 00:03:02,661
she went out of her way to go do laundry,

43
00:03:02,661 --> 00:03:05,145
just to talk to you.

44
00:03:06,077 --> 00:03:10,853
But we've lived together in the same house,
just the two or three of us,

45
00:03:10,853 --> 00:03:13,978
and I think I understand Azusa pretty well.

46
00:03:17,257 --> 00:03:19,097
What? Really?

47
00:03:20,073 --> 00:03:22,645
Did you really ask Azusa to go with you?

48
00:03:26,633 --> 00:03:28,373
Yes.

49
00:03:29,145 --> 00:03:32,895
It was all my fault. I dragged her into it.

50
00:04:07,133 --> 00:04:09,186
Hello, it's me.

51
00:04:09,959 --> 00:04:11,309
Thank you for everything.

52
00:04:11,309 --> 00:04:14,257
Ah, your ride is here.

53
00:04:14,257 --> 00:04:16,841
Wait just a second, Daddy.
I'm at a good part.

54
00:04:16,841 --> 00:04:19,373
I'm sorry, we're right in the middle
of a good part here.

55
00:04:19,373 --> 00:04:22,017
Okay, I'll wait.

56
00:04:22,017 --> 00:04:26,977
The tail goes whoosh, and then smack!

57
00:04:27,977 --> 00:04:30,153
It's fun, right?

58
00:04:31,473 --> 00:04:34,241
Sorry, I'll be waiting outside.

59
00:04:34,241 --> 00:04:36,185
Okay.

60
00:04:40,945 --> 00:04:42,729
I'm home.

61
00:04:49,301 --> 00:04:50,914
Sacchan.

62
00:04:51,566 --> 00:04:53,311
I'm back.

63
00:05:00,881 --> 00:05:02,953
Sacchan?

64
00:05:02,953 --> 00:05:06,228
Sacchan? Sacchan!

65
00:05:10,813 --> 00:05:12,895
What's wrong?

66
00:05:15,933 --> 00:05:17,749
Oh, you were in the bathroom?

67
00:05:17,749 --> 00:05:20,041
Yeah.

68
00:05:20,041 --> 00:05:23,397
Huh? Why were you panicking? What happened?

69
00:05:23,397 --> 00:05:25,053
Did someone hit you? Do you need ice?

70
00:05:25,053 --> 00:05:27,509
Nobody hit me. I'm fine.

71
00:05:27,509 --> 00:05:30,601
What is it? What happened?

72
00:05:30,601 --> 00:05:32,603
I thought you were gone.

73
00:05:34,405 --> 00:05:36,225
What?

74
00:05:38,505 --> 00:05:41,777
You were panicking
because you thought I was gone?

75
00:05:41,777 --> 00:05:43,161
Yeah.

76
00:05:43,161 --> 00:05:46,617
Just because you couldn't
find me for a few seconds?

77
00:05:47,537 --> 00:05:49,377
Yeah.

78
00:05:52,749 --> 00:05:57,229
Someone who disappeared for a year
doesn't have any right to worry like that.

79
00:05:57,229 --> 00:05:59,085
I'm sorry.

80
00:06:02,641 --> 00:06:04,449
I'm sorry.

81
00:06:06,505 --> 00:06:09,561
I'm the one who suspected you,
so I have no right, but...

82
00:06:11,821 --> 00:06:15,449
I even thought that maybe it would have been
better if you had been cheating.

83
00:06:16,589 --> 00:06:20,228
Because inviting her to go with you
for something like that...

84
00:06:21,229 --> 00:06:23,645
That's a pretty
deep level of trust, isn't it?

85
00:06:24,553 --> 00:06:26,925
When we got married, we decided

86
00:06:26,925 --> 00:06:30,061
that it's okay to not talk about things
we don't want to.

87
00:06:30,757 --> 00:06:32,077
Yeah.

88
00:06:32,077 --> 00:06:34,070
If I told you, you might hate me.

89
00:06:34,070 --> 00:06:36,873
There are a lot of things
I'm too scared to say.

90
00:06:36,873 --> 00:06:39,505
But I'll tell you. Otherwise...

91
00:06:39,505 --> 00:06:41,273
I can't explain why I ran away,

92
00:06:41,273 --> 00:06:44,811
or what happened with Mrs. Sonoda.

93
00:06:51,669 --> 00:06:53,936
I'll go get the laundry.

94
00:06:55,013 --> 00:06:56,841
Yeah.

95
00:07:00,469 --> 00:07:04,929
That there, I gave it to him. It's Azusa's.

96
00:07:04,929 --> 00:07:06,873
- Here.
- Oh, yes.

97
00:07:06,873 --> 00:07:10,701
I gave this to Azusa.

98
00:07:10,701 --> 00:07:14,197
Before we got married.
Maybe it was on her birthday?

99
00:07:14,197 --> 00:07:16,353
Yeah.

100
00:07:16,353 --> 00:07:18,829
It matches the book cover.

101
00:07:18,829 --> 00:07:20,686
Wait, how do you know?

102
00:07:22,229 --> 00:07:23,978
Ah.

103
00:07:25,689 --> 00:07:29,705
She said she really liked these.
That bookmark, and also this.

104
00:07:29,705 --> 00:07:32,113
I never saw it at home.

105
00:07:32,113 --> 00:07:34,353
I thought she'd lost it.

106
00:07:36,493 --> 00:07:38,553
Then take it home.

107
00:07:38,553 --> 00:07:40,433
Thank you.

108
00:07:41,973 --> 00:07:45,257
Her things just keep turning up, don't they?

109
00:07:45,257 --> 00:07:47,277
Even after a year.

110
00:07:52,941 --> 00:07:54,771
[Sho stubbed his little toe
on the table leg and was hurting]

111
00:07:54,771 --> 00:07:57,665
[I witnessed Sho's first time stubbing his
little toe on a table leg and being in pain]

112
00:07:57,665 --> 00:08:00,433
Since that husband seems to be back,

113
00:08:00,433 --> 00:08:02,933
I thought it would be okay
for me to give this to you now.

114
00:08:04,113 --> 00:08:06,105
No, no, you should look at it.

115
00:08:06,105 --> 00:08:09,909
There wasn't anything written in there
that would shock you.

116
00:08:09,909 --> 00:08:13,103
Oh sorry, I read it.

117
00:08:16,845 --> 00:08:20,978
I know now that she wasn't having an affair.

118
00:08:22,609 --> 00:08:27,217
But... that's actually why I'm shocked.

119
00:08:27,217 --> 00:08:28,481
Huh?

120
00:08:28,481 --> 00:08:33,133
A trust that's separate
from romantic feelings...

121
00:08:33,133 --> 00:08:35,561
<i>Isn't that just too strong?</i>

122
00:08:42,325 --> 00:08:45,770
How much have I told you about my parents?

123
00:08:47,377 --> 00:08:50,885
That you went to a high school
that had a dormitory for its swimming club,

124
00:08:50,885 --> 00:08:53,801
and after that, you and your parents
gradually grew apart. That's all.

125
00:08:53,801 --> 00:08:55,337
Yeah.

126
00:08:55,337 --> 00:08:56,812
The last time I saw them

127
00:08:56,812 --> 00:08:59,993
was reluctantly when
I got into college, for scholarship stuff.

128
00:08:59,993 --> 00:09:03,270
So it's been about twenty years.

129
00:09:05,961 --> 00:09:07,957
I see.

130
00:09:07,957 --> 00:09:12,061
My parents didn't want kids.

131
00:09:12,061 --> 00:09:15,895
But they had me,
so they got married, I guess.

132
00:09:16,584 --> 00:09:18,257
I didn't hear that directly.

133
00:09:18,257 --> 00:09:21,745
I just overheard relatives
talking about it over drinks.

134
00:09:21,745 --> 00:09:24,937
Kids can tell, you know,
when adults are like that.

135
00:09:32,653 --> 00:09:35,841
- Hello.
- Hello.

136
00:09:35,841 --> 00:09:38,603
Mitsuru, you're looking well.

137
00:09:40,053 --> 00:09:43,003
<i>Sometimes there are poor kids</i>

138
00:09:43,003 --> 00:09:45,501
<i>who need adults other than their parents
to protect them,</i>

139
00:09:45,501 --> 00:09:47,521
<i>but I wasn't like that.</i>

140
00:09:49,557 --> 00:09:51,811
<i>I had food to eat.</i>

141
00:09:53,521 --> 00:09:55,436
<i>I could take baths.</i>

142
00:09:58,353 --> 00:10:00,686
<i>My clothes were washed.</i>

143
00:10:01,885 --> 00:10:04,125
<i>I wasn't hit or kicked.</i>

144
00:10:04,897 --> 00:10:08,686
I just... wasn't loved.

145
00:10:11,145 --> 00:10:14,765
Not loving your child isn't against the law.

146
00:10:14,765 --> 00:10:16,766
So I lived with my parents.

147
00:10:18,453 --> 00:10:21,341
<i>Until nursery school, I wanted attention,</i>

148
00:10:21,341 --> 00:10:23,889
<i>so I acted out
and tried my best to get attention.</i>

149
00:10:26,001 --> 00:10:28,549
<i>But they never got angry.</i>

150
00:10:28,549 --> 00:10:32,103
<i>They'd just sigh or glare.</i>

151
00:10:33,285 --> 00:10:34,912
<i>After I started elementary school,</i>

152
00:10:34,912 --> 00:10:38,393
<i>I made friends and listened to my teachers.</i>

153
00:10:38,393 --> 00:10:42,713
<i>If I was a good kid, my mom wouldn't sigh,</i>

154
00:10:42,713 --> 00:10:44,413
and she wouldn't glare.

155
00:10:46,825 --> 00:10:50,505
The thing I was most surprised
to learn at school

156
00:10:50,505 --> 00:10:54,349
was that birthdays
are meant to be celebrated.

157
00:10:54,349 --> 00:10:56,377
When did you learn that?

158
00:10:56,377 --> 00:10:58,936
Did you have candles on a cake
at home for your birthday?

159
00:11:01,953 --> 00:11:04,117
I was born in Tokyo, apparently,

160
00:11:04,117 --> 00:11:06,494
but because of my dad's job,
ever since I could remember,

161
00:11:06,494 --> 00:11:08,941
we moved very frequently.

162
00:11:08,941 --> 00:11:11,345
I don't even know how many times
I transferred schools.

163
00:11:13,273 --> 00:11:15,561
I remember you said that
you didn't have a family home.

164
00:11:16,677 --> 00:11:20,829
No hometown, no childhood friends,
nothing like that.

165
00:11:20,829 --> 00:11:23,686
And no fixed place to go back to either.

166
00:11:25,165 --> 00:11:28,193
- That must have been hard.
- Not really.

167
00:11:28,193 --> 00:11:30,897
It was actually good
that it was that kind of family.

168
00:11:32,865 --> 00:11:37,241
In the town we moved to
in the winter of first grade,

169
00:11:37,241 --> 00:11:40,565
there was a coffee shop that my mom liked.

170
00:11:40,565 --> 00:11:42,693
<i>When she was in a good mood, she'd take me.</i>

171
00:11:42,693 --> 00:11:45,513
Mom, the cake was delicious.

172
00:11:45,513 --> 00:11:48,853
There were things
other than strawberries in it too.

173
00:11:53,825 --> 00:11:56,321
I'm going to the bathroom.

174
00:12:06,857 --> 00:12:08,549
Do you like trains?

175
00:12:08,549 --> 00:12:10,297
Yes.

176
00:12:14,897 --> 00:12:16,649
Here.

177
00:12:19,313 --> 00:12:22,895
I'm not giving it to you,
but you can look at it and touch it.

178
00:12:24,813 --> 00:12:27,677
There were things
other than strawberries in the cake.

179
00:12:27,677 --> 00:12:30,270
Yeah, like kiwi and orange.

180
00:12:31,513 --> 00:12:34,657
Even though it was a strawberry shortcake?

181
00:12:34,657 --> 00:12:36,989
[Today's Cake: Strawberry Shortcake]
A strawberry, kiwi, and orange shortcake.

182
00:12:36,989 --> 00:12:38,677
Saying all that would be a mouthful, right?

183
00:12:38,677 --> 00:12:41,520
Right? And it'd also be a pain
to write all that out.

184
00:12:42,607 --> 00:12:47,213
<i>That shop owner was an adult
who listened to me.</i>

185
00:12:48,849 --> 00:12:51,270
I told him everything.
Things my mom wouldn't listen to,

186
00:12:52,077 --> 00:12:53,745
<i>and things I couldn't tell my teacher</i>

187
00:12:53,745 --> 00:12:58,349
<i>because I was trying to be a good kid.</i>

188
00:12:58,349 --> 00:13:03,069
That I rooted for the villains
more than the heroes in anime.

189
00:13:03,685 --> 00:13:05,517
That to make sure
I wouldn't eat too much at home,

190
00:13:05,517 --> 00:13:08,821
<i>I'd get seconds at school lunch,
even when I was full.</i>

191
00:13:08,821 --> 00:13:10,181
<i>That I'd miss my stop on purpose</i>

192
00:13:10,181 --> 00:13:13,673
<i>and go on adventures to the bus stop
two stops further away.</i>

193
00:13:15,681 --> 00:13:18,686
- Here you go.
- Thank you.

194
00:13:21,821 --> 00:13:24,311
- Delicious.
- Delicious?

195
00:13:26,261 --> 00:13:27,733
<i>When it was decided that
we would be moving again...</i>

196
00:13:27,733 --> 00:13:30,736
[Thank you
Mitsuru Seo]

197
00:13:30,736 --> 00:13:31,855
on the day before we moved,

198
00:13:31,855 --> 00:13:35,145
I went alone to the coffee shop
and told the owner.

199
00:13:36,505 --> 00:13:43,277
You don't need to be attached
to one place or one person.

200
00:13:43,277 --> 00:13:45,953
From now on, you can go anywhere.

201
00:13:45,953 --> 00:13:48,337
You'll meet all kinds of people.

202
00:13:48,337 --> 00:13:50,419
He didn't seem sad.

203
00:13:50,419 --> 00:13:53,969
I thought he was a bit cold-hearted,
but it also made me feel better.

204
00:13:53,969 --> 00:13:55,697
[Thank you
Mitsuru Seo]

205
00:13:58,573 --> 00:14:01,153
<i>That I didn't have to stay in one place.</i>

206
00:14:03,189 --> 00:14:04,895
That night...

207
00:14:06,085 --> 00:14:08,269
I ran away for the first time in my life.

208
00:14:09,421 --> 00:14:11,093
You ran away?

209
00:14:11,093 --> 00:14:15,557
I just took random trains
to go as far away as I could.

210
00:14:15,557 --> 00:14:17,234
And after the last train had left,

211
00:14:17,234 --> 00:14:19,845
I slept on a bench in a park
in a strange town.

212
00:14:19,845 --> 00:14:23,613
I hitched a ride on a truck
and crossed prefectural borders.

213
00:14:24,221 --> 00:14:26,073
Well, I was just eight years old.

214
00:14:26,073 --> 00:14:29,645
People thought I got lost
and took me into protective custody.

215
00:14:30,645 --> 00:14:33,029
My parents didn't get mad.

216
00:14:33,029 --> 00:14:34,217
They were just worried about appearances,

217
00:14:34,217 --> 00:14:36,753
and told me not to cause any more trouble.

218
00:14:36,753 --> 00:14:42,157
But the thrill of going far away
without anyone knowing, without a purpose...

219
00:14:42,157 --> 00:14:44,025
I couldn't stop.

220
00:14:44,841 --> 00:14:48,117
During a field trip in fourth grade,

221
00:14:48,117 --> 00:14:51,345
during a school trip
to the beach in sixth grade,

222
00:14:51,345 --> 00:14:53,753
during a club trip in eighth grade,

223
00:14:53,753 --> 00:14:56,689
in the middle of a date
with my girlfriend in eleventh grade,

224
00:14:56,689 --> 00:15:00,545
in my first year of college,
the morning after summer break...

225
00:15:00,545 --> 00:15:03,919
I'd just vanish
and go completely off the grid.

226
00:15:04,833 --> 00:15:08,021
In terms of feelings,
it's a desire to escape.

227
00:15:08,021 --> 00:15:12,661
In modern terms, it's like
'relationship reset syndrome.'

228
00:15:12,661 --> 00:15:14,728
The easiest way to put it is

229
00:15:15,373 --> 00:15:17,197
a 'disappearing habit.'

230
00:15:18,297 --> 00:15:20,197
Disappearing habit...

231
00:15:20,197 --> 00:15:23,233
That's why I was grateful
for my family's constant moving.

232
00:15:23,233 --> 00:15:26,145
Because I didn't have to belong
to a community for long.

233
00:15:26,961 --> 00:15:29,205
But family is strange, isn't it?

234
00:15:29,205 --> 00:15:32,436
Why do they keep living together
even after moving?

235
00:15:34,733 --> 00:15:37,653
I'd run away every two or three years,

236
00:15:37,653 --> 00:15:40,929
and when I grew up,
I'd switch jobs each time.

237
00:15:40,929 --> 00:15:42,445
Right after I met you,

238
00:15:42,445 --> 00:15:44,689
there was a time when
I went off the grid, remember?

239
00:15:45,961 --> 00:15:49,325
Yeah, about three months.

240
00:15:49,981 --> 00:15:52,485
You said it was for a business trip abroad.

241
00:15:52,485 --> 00:15:54,285
That was a lie.

242
00:15:55,641 --> 00:15:58,729
That was my last disappearance,
until this one.

243
00:15:58,729 --> 00:16:00,949
By then I'd already quit my previous job

244
00:16:00,949 --> 00:16:03,349
and reset all my relationships.

245
00:16:04,533 --> 00:16:07,145
Finding a new job was getting harder,

246
00:16:07,145 --> 00:16:10,905
so I thought I'd do something on my own,
and that's the current coffee shop.

247
00:16:10,905 --> 00:16:13,173
I told you I wanted to own my own shop,

248
00:16:13,173 --> 00:16:15,565
so I lied about quitting my previous job.

249
00:16:15,565 --> 00:16:18,145
But actually, it was the other way around.

250
00:16:18,805 --> 00:16:21,311
I had no idea.

251
00:16:22,453 --> 00:16:25,973
I suppose I'm probably a good liar.

252
00:16:25,973 --> 00:16:29,385
Because ever since I can remember,
everything was a lie.

253
00:16:29,385 --> 00:16:31,225
It was before we got married,

254
00:16:32,149 --> 00:16:34,228
but I'm sorry for the lie.

255
00:16:38,913 --> 00:16:41,573
I'm such an idiot.

256
00:16:43,153 --> 00:16:47,173
During the time I couldn't reach you,
I was lonely,

257
00:16:47,173 --> 00:16:51,087
and I became sure that I really loved you.

258
00:16:52,449 --> 00:16:54,529
That's what I thought during that time.

259
00:16:54,529 --> 00:16:56,609
Me too.

260
00:16:56,609 --> 00:16:58,653
But you were the one who disappeared.

261
00:16:58,653 --> 00:17:00,625
When I run away,

262
00:17:00,625 --> 00:17:04,585
I aim for places
where I won't see anyone I know.

263
00:17:04,585 --> 00:17:08,277
But that time, for the first time,
I thought, I want to see her.

264
00:17:09,393 --> 00:17:12,709
Sakiko Nakabayashi...
I wonder how she's doing?

265
00:17:12,709 --> 00:17:16,253
I ignored your messages for three months,
so I thought you might hate me.

266
00:17:16,253 --> 00:17:17,961
I got anxious.

267
00:17:18,757 --> 00:17:21,073
For the first time, I wanted to see someone.

268
00:17:24,281 --> 00:17:26,561
"Sorry for the delay."

269
00:17:27,373 --> 00:17:29,936
"I just got back from
an overseas business trip."

270
00:17:30,641 --> 00:17:33,311
"I'd love to see you again."

271
00:17:35,033 --> 00:17:38,645
You remember a LINE message
from ten years ago?

272
00:17:38,645 --> 00:17:40,497
Yes, I remember.

273
00:17:43,029 --> 00:17:45,265
I was so happy I took a screenshot.

274
00:17:46,873 --> 00:17:48,709
I'm sorry about the lie.

275
00:17:48,709 --> 00:17:50,645
Wanting to see you was real, though.

276
00:17:56,297 --> 00:17:59,311
Let's put this away. Want to take a bath?

277
00:18:00,581 --> 00:18:03,289
Okay, but I want to hear everything first.

278
00:18:03,289 --> 00:18:05,169
Okay.

279
00:18:30,849 --> 00:18:31,909
[April 28 (Sun)
While I was folding the laundry,]

280
00:18:31,909 --> 00:18:32,981
[Sho said on his own that
he wanted to help out.]

281
00:18:32,981 --> 00:18:34,013
[A good man, just like his dad.]

282
00:18:34,013 --> 00:18:35,125
[Seems he has a crush at nursery school.
He won't tell me, but it's probably Moka.]

283
00:18:35,125 --> 00:18:36,229
[Happy, but also a bit complicated.]

284
00:18:36,229 --> 00:18:37,393
[Right after waking up, he said,
"Morning! Yesterday was so fun."]

285
00:18:37,393 --> 00:18:38,593
[I had no idea what he was talking about,
but apparently he went swimming in his dream.]

286
00:18:38,593 --> 00:18:40,451
[He had fun because he got three ice creams
on the way back from swimming...]

287
00:18:50,565 --> 00:18:52,395
That's Mom's handwriting.

288
00:18:55,829 --> 00:18:58,465
- It's Mom's handwriting, right? Read it.
- Yeah. What's wrong?

289
00:18:58,465 --> 00:19:00,605
Did you wake up? Need the bathroom?

290
00:19:01,301 --> 00:19:03,673
- Read it, what Mom wrote.
- Okay.

291
00:19:04,617 --> 00:19:06,901
Later, okay?

292
00:19:06,901 --> 00:19:08,773
Let's go back to sleep for now.

293
00:19:08,773 --> 00:19:10,606
Will you read it later?

294
00:19:11,809 --> 00:19:14,865
Yeah, I'll read it later.

295
00:19:16,581 --> 00:19:19,209
I turned on the AC in the bedroom.

296
00:19:20,473 --> 00:19:22,149
Hey.

297
00:19:22,149 --> 00:19:25,125
You said just now that you'd run away
every two or three years.

298
00:19:25,125 --> 00:19:27,185
Yeah.

299
00:19:27,185 --> 00:19:31,809
But it's been about ten years since then.

300
00:19:31,809 --> 00:19:33,593
We started dating pretty soon after that,

301
00:19:33,593 --> 00:19:36,645
got married, and have been
together ever since.

302
00:19:37,289 --> 00:19:39,657
You're amazing, Sacchan.

303
00:19:39,657 --> 00:19:42,356
You kept me in the same place for ten years.

304
00:19:43,709 --> 00:19:45,597
Then why did you do it again?

305
00:19:46,565 --> 00:19:48,356
A child.

306
00:19:51,741 --> 00:19:54,121
We realized it would be
difficult to have one,

307
00:19:54,121 --> 00:19:58,261
so we decided to stop treatment
and buy a house, right? You wanted that.

308
00:19:59,397 --> 00:20:03,669
- Yeah.
- I'd never lived in a house before.

309
00:20:03,669 --> 00:20:07,929
So I was looking forward to it,
and it was fun deciding things together.

310
00:20:07,929 --> 00:20:09,981
Wow!

311
00:20:10,729 --> 00:20:12,673
This feels good.

312
00:20:13,589 --> 00:20:19,148
<i>But the moment I stepped inside, I thought,</i>

313
00:20:20,125 --> 00:20:24,189
it's going to be the two of us here forever.
I can't run away anymore.

314
00:20:25,809 --> 00:20:27,817
I thought it was just my imagination,

315
00:20:27,817 --> 00:20:31,277
but once we started living here,
I wanted to run away more and more.

316
00:20:31,277 --> 00:20:33,641
I'd get on the wrong bus or train on purpose,

317
00:20:33,641 --> 00:20:36,405
trying to get taken somewhere I didn't know.

318
00:20:36,405 --> 00:20:40,023
There were signs like that, and it scared me.

319
00:20:41,433 --> 00:20:43,773
It's not like I dislike you.

320
00:20:44,823 --> 00:20:48,189
But just the two of us,
in the same place forever...

321
00:20:48,189 --> 00:20:50,269
The more I thought about it,

322
00:20:51,369 --> 00:20:53,761
the more I felt suffocated by happiness.

323
00:20:58,333 --> 00:21:00,997
Even if I explain it,
it probably doesn't make sense.

324
00:21:00,997 --> 00:21:03,531
Even if I say I don't hate you,

325
00:21:03,531 --> 00:21:06,689
you'd suspect that would mean
that I love someone else.

326
00:21:07,345 --> 00:21:11,564
It's always sudden.
There's no planning for tomorrow.

327
00:21:12,465 --> 00:21:15,537
That day was the same, at the service area.

328
00:21:15,537 --> 00:21:18,481
I thought, now's the time.
I'll just disappear.

329
00:21:19,701 --> 00:21:22,809
I never thought the bus
would get into an accident.

330
00:21:22,809 --> 00:21:25,081
I took advantage of Mrs. Sonoda
and got off alone.

331
00:21:26,413 --> 00:21:29,481
- I don't think you'll understand, but...
- I understand.

332
00:21:32,033 --> 00:21:34,981
I understand that you're that kind of person.

333
00:21:35,773 --> 00:21:37,898
I understand your past too.

334
00:21:39,817 --> 00:21:41,648
And I believe all of it.

335
00:21:44,825 --> 00:21:46,565
Yeah.

336
00:21:47,553 --> 00:21:49,293
So...

337
00:21:51,549 --> 00:21:53,856
What should I have done?

338
00:21:56,173 --> 00:21:58,345
What should I have done
to make you come back sooner?

339
00:21:58,345 --> 00:22:01,221
You've never done anything wrong.

340
00:22:01,221 --> 00:22:02,801
With everyone I've met before,

341
00:22:02,801 --> 00:22:06,129
I've always assumed that
the connection would eventually break.

342
00:22:06,129 --> 00:22:07,898
But you were different.

343
00:22:09,885 --> 00:22:12,106
I didn't know what to do.

344
00:22:13,125 --> 00:22:17,545
I want to be with you,
but the real me is like this.

345
00:22:17,545 --> 00:22:19,856
I was always scared you'd hate me,
and you'd disappear.

346
00:22:21,597 --> 00:22:23,314
I was always scared.

347
00:22:25,161 --> 00:22:28,148
So you'd rather disappear yourself
before that happens?

348
00:22:29,813 --> 00:22:32,106
I started thinking that way,

349
00:22:32,805 --> 00:22:34,689
but I managed not to run away.

350
00:22:36,829 --> 00:22:38,601
Why?

351
00:22:39,961 --> 00:22:42,439
Because on the third Friday of every month,

352
00:22:43,433 --> 00:22:45,314
I could see Mrs. Sonoda.

353
00:23:00,129 --> 00:23:05,106
<i>Friday, June 21, 2024.</i>

354
00:23:06,637 --> 00:23:10,217
<i>I wanted to wash all my summer clothes
that smelled musty from the closet,</i>

355
00:23:10,217 --> 00:23:12,898
<i>so I went to a nearby laundromat.</i>

356
00:23:13,705 --> 00:23:17,564
<i>That's where I met Mr. Seo.</i>

357
00:23:18,197 --> 00:23:20,533
About a year before the accident,

358
00:23:20,533 --> 00:23:23,601
I wanted to wash all the stuff from the shop,

359
00:23:23,601 --> 00:23:26,321
so I went to a nearby laundromat,

360
00:23:26,321 --> 00:23:30,939
and that's where I met Mrs. Sonoda.

361
00:23:43,961 --> 00:23:45,721
What next?

362
00:23:51,137 --> 00:23:53,497
Excuse me.

363
00:23:53,497 --> 00:23:57,561
Well... how do I operate this?

364
00:23:57,561 --> 00:23:59,633
I want to dry some clothes.

365
00:24:01,247 --> 00:24:04,313
- With cash?
- Oh, yes, cash.

366
00:24:04,313 --> 00:24:05,829
- So you put the money in here...
- And then, the money.

367
00:24:05,829 --> 00:24:08,549
- I see. Thank you.
- No problem.

368
00:24:14,089 --> 00:24:16,113
Do you come here often?

369
00:24:17,417 --> 00:24:18,997
- Me?
- Yes.

370
00:24:18,997 --> 00:24:20,525
No, it's my first time here.

371
00:24:20,525 --> 00:24:22,622
Did your washing machine break?

372
00:24:22,622 --> 00:24:25,297
No, I'm just washing some things
that I use for work.

373
00:24:25,297 --> 00:24:26,497
And there was quite a lot.

374
00:24:26,497 --> 00:24:29,861
Would your wife get mad
if you washed stuff from work at home?

375
00:24:29,861 --> 00:24:33,477
No, she wouldn't get mad.
It's just that there was a lot to wash.

376
00:24:33,477 --> 00:24:35,198
I see.

377
00:24:36,141 --> 00:24:37,621
How did you know that I'm married?

378
00:24:37,621 --> 00:24:39,349
Oh, the ring.

379
00:24:58,653 --> 00:25:00,530
Are you reading a book?

380
00:25:01,165 --> 00:25:03,688
- Yes.
- I thought so.

381
00:25:05,309 --> 00:25:07,705
You mean, is it okay if you talk to me?

382
00:25:07,705 --> 00:25:08,925
Yes.

383
00:25:08,925 --> 00:25:11,309
I'm just reading to kill time, so it's fine.

384
00:25:11,309 --> 00:25:14,177
So, is it okay if we chat?

385
00:25:14,177 --> 00:25:16,697
Okay, until my laundry is done.

386
00:25:16,697 --> 00:25:18,537
Thank you.

387
00:25:18,537 --> 00:25:21,369
Okay, what next? What should we talk about?

388
00:25:21,369 --> 00:25:24,241
<i>- Mrs. Sonoda was a strange person.</i>
- Hmm, what to talk about?

389
00:25:24,241 --> 00:25:25,769
<i>She wanted to talk to a stranger</i>

390
00:25:25,769 --> 00:25:27,473
<i>- she didn't need to talk to.</i>
- What to talk about?

391
00:25:27,473 --> 00:25:29,337
<i>She went out of her way to chat.</i>

392
00:25:29,337 --> 00:25:30,933
<i>I didn't get it.</i>

393
00:25:30,933 --> 00:25:33,153
Let's see.

394
00:25:33,153 --> 00:25:36,305
Something I can't tell my husband
would be good.

395
00:25:36,305 --> 00:25:38,593
I usually tell my husband everything.

396
00:25:38,593 --> 00:25:40,317
- I see.
- Ah.

397
00:25:40,317 --> 00:25:43,017
How much would you spend on a T-shirt?

398
00:25:43,017 --> 00:25:45,613
You know, a plain white one.

399
00:25:46,433 --> 00:25:49,033
- Maybe 3,000 yen?
- Right, right.

400
00:25:49,033 --> 00:25:53,108
My husband would spend up to 15,000 yen
on a white T-shirt.

401
00:25:53,108 --> 00:25:55,065
What do you think?

402
00:25:55,065 --> 00:25:58,049
Well, I think it's up to you
where you spend your money.

403
00:25:58,049 --> 00:26:00,425
If it's not hurting the household budget,

404
00:26:00,425 --> 00:26:02,021
- and it's just your husband's preference...
- No, no, it's fine.

405
00:26:02,021 --> 00:26:03,663
This is just chitchat.

406
00:26:03,663 --> 00:26:07,363
This isn't work or school.
Give me your honest opinion.

407
00:26:10,549 --> 00:26:14,381
We probably won't see each other again.

408
00:26:14,381 --> 00:26:15,498
Even in the worst case scenario,

409
00:26:15,498 --> 00:26:17,865
being hated wouldn't really matter either.

410
00:26:17,865 --> 00:26:20,497
Would you feel hurt if I hated you?

411
00:26:20,497 --> 00:26:22,445
- No.
- See?

412
00:26:23,345 --> 00:26:28,381
That's why I love talking to people
who have nothing to do with my life.

413
00:26:30,389 --> 00:26:33,747
I think 15,000 yen is really expensive.

414
00:26:33,747 --> 00:26:36,853
See? You do think it's expensive.

415
00:26:36,853 --> 00:26:39,105
Don't lie to me.

416
00:26:39,105 --> 00:26:41,165
Sorry.

417
00:26:41,165 --> 00:26:44,069
And, as you'd expect from spending that much,

418
00:26:44,069 --> 00:26:46,109
he's really particular about it.

419
00:26:46,109 --> 00:26:47,425
There are so many shades of white.

420
00:26:47,425 --> 00:26:49,297
You know that famous quote, right?

421
00:26:49,297 --> 00:26:52,789
Itsuki says it with a straight face,
not as a joke.

422
00:26:52,789 --> 00:26:54,453
Oh right, my husband's name is Itsuki.

423
00:26:54,453 --> 00:26:55,697
And then...

424
00:26:55,697 --> 00:26:58,477
<i>Mrs. Sonoda was definitely a strange person.</i>

425
00:26:58,477 --> 00:27:02,401
<i>Whatever came to mind,
it just came out of her mouth.</i>

426
00:27:02,401 --> 00:27:05,530
<i>She was like a completely
different creature from me.</i>

427
00:27:06,401 --> 00:27:09,341
<i>Mr. Seo was a strange person.</i>

428
00:27:09,341 --> 00:27:13,225
<i>He listened seriously to my silly stories,</i>

429
00:27:13,225 --> 00:27:16,437
<i>but everything he said seemed like a lie.</i>

430
00:27:16,437 --> 00:27:20,693
I've never objected to Itsuki
spending 15,000 yen on a white T-shirt.

431
00:27:20,693 --> 00:27:23,713
Not even once.

432
00:27:23,713 --> 00:27:25,655
And the reason for that is...

433
00:27:27,841 --> 00:27:30,089
Well, the reason is...

434
00:27:30,089 --> 00:27:32,293
- Huh?
- No.

435
00:27:32,965 --> 00:27:35,325
Itsuki's only hobby...

436
00:27:39,781 --> 00:27:41,965
is taking walks.

437
00:27:41,965 --> 00:27:43,645
- Really?
- I mean,

438
00:27:43,645 --> 00:27:46,145
a man in his mid-thirties
whose hobby is walking...

439
00:27:46,145 --> 00:27:48,069
Wouldn't you think that he's boring?

440
00:27:48,069 --> 00:27:49,469
What's wrong with walking?

441
00:27:49,469 --> 00:27:51,697
Right? It's nice.

442
00:27:52,749 --> 00:27:56,453
It's free, and it's good for one's health.

443
00:27:56,453 --> 00:27:59,457
He takes Sho to the park and stuff.

444
00:27:59,457 --> 00:28:01,425
Sho is my son, he's four.

445
00:28:01,425 --> 00:28:02,881
Sho, like Shohei Ohtani.

446
00:28:02,881 --> 00:28:05,301
And when he goes to the park with Sho...

447
00:28:05,301 --> 00:28:08,038
<i>Even when I thought it was going to be
a complaint about her husband,</i>

448
00:28:08,038 --> 00:28:11,309
<i>it always ended up being a lovey-dovey story.</i>

449
00:28:11,309 --> 00:28:14,941
<i>I think she really loves her husband
and can't help it.</i>

450
00:28:14,941 --> 00:28:19,113
Oh, do you have weekdays off? Today's Friday.

451
00:28:19,113 --> 00:28:22,288
I run a coffee shop
and Fridays are my regular day off.

452
00:28:22,288 --> 00:28:24,885
Wow, that's nice, having your own shop.

453
00:28:24,885 --> 00:28:28,097
I work part-time at a used bookstore,

454
00:28:28,097 --> 00:28:32,205
and on Fridays, another part-timer can cover,

455
00:28:32,205 --> 00:28:34,155
so I usually have the day off.

456
00:28:34,897 --> 00:28:37,193
I love having days off on weekdays.

457
00:28:37,193 --> 00:28:40,677
Itsuki's at work,
and Sho's at nursery school.

458
00:28:40,677 --> 00:28:43,780
I love the time we spend together
as a family of three,

459
00:28:44,465 --> 00:28:46,353
but that's probably

460
00:28:46,353 --> 00:28:50,437
because I can get away from them on Fridays.

461
00:28:50,437 --> 00:28:53,530
Ah... I get it.

462
00:28:54,877 --> 00:28:57,145
Well, maybe this is it.

463
00:28:57,145 --> 00:28:59,273
I want to be with Sacchan, and it's fun,

464
00:28:59,273 --> 00:29:01,341
but being together all the time is hard.

465
00:29:01,341 --> 00:29:03,947
That's why I don't want to change my day off.

466
00:29:05,245 --> 00:29:07,085
- Oh.
- Sacchan...

467
00:29:07,085 --> 00:29:08,605
Your wife?

468
00:29:08,605 --> 00:29:10,745
- Yes.
- Huh?

469
00:29:10,745 --> 00:29:13,365
Why do you look so guilty?

470
00:29:13,365 --> 00:29:14,729
- No...
- No, I mean,

471
00:29:14,729 --> 00:29:17,869
it's normal to feel that way about your wife.

472
00:29:19,349 --> 00:29:24,141
I feel that way not just
about my husband, but my kid too.

473
00:29:24,141 --> 00:29:26,481
Sometimes I just want to get away.

474
00:29:26,481 --> 00:29:28,433
Even though I love them so much.

475
00:29:29,057 --> 00:29:31,109
Is that how it is?

476
00:29:31,109 --> 00:29:33,225
Yes.

477
00:29:33,225 --> 00:29:34,993
I see.

478
00:29:37,461 --> 00:29:39,947
What's it like being a parent?

479
00:29:43,797 --> 00:29:46,363
Sho...

480
00:29:47,145 --> 00:29:51,993
He's very precious to me, and I love him.

481
00:29:53,213 --> 00:29:55,189
I'm glad I had him.

482
00:30:01,125 --> 00:30:02,437
Want something to drink?

483
00:30:02,437 --> 00:30:04,521
No, I'm fine.

484
00:30:14,581 --> 00:30:19,497
I don't have parents, so I lack role models.

485
00:30:19,497 --> 00:30:22,649
Oh, but that does have its own advantages.

486
00:30:22,649 --> 00:30:27,481
Having a clear idea of what a perfect mother
is supposed to be must be tough too.

487
00:30:27,481 --> 00:30:31,573
I'm basically parentless too, so I get it.

488
00:30:31,573 --> 00:30:33,681
Did they pass away?
Or were you in an institution?

489
00:30:33,681 --> 00:30:35,405
No, they're around,

490
00:30:35,405 --> 00:30:38,197
but they were indifferent to me as a kid.

491
00:30:39,941 --> 00:30:43,277
I was dumped the moment I was born.

492
00:30:43,277 --> 00:30:46,965
So I was in an institution
until I graduated from high school.

493
00:30:46,965 --> 00:30:51,569
I'm a little jealous of that.

494
00:30:51,569 --> 00:30:54,389
Oh, you said what you really think!

495
00:30:54,389 --> 00:30:57,593
- Sorry.
- No, no, it's fine.

496
00:30:57,593 --> 00:31:01,741
It's way better than
getting that 'poor you' vibe.

497
00:31:01,741 --> 00:31:04,669
I mean, compared to being raised
by crappy parents,

498
00:31:04,669 --> 00:31:07,405
I've had a super happy life.

499
00:31:07,405 --> 00:31:09,197
You seem happy.

500
00:31:10,617 --> 00:31:15,363
In an institution,
there are fewer adults than kids,

501
00:31:16,033 --> 00:31:20,295
so I didn't get enough of
"Hey, Mom, listen to this."

502
00:31:20,295 --> 00:31:21,941
Probably more so than kids in regular homes.

503
00:31:21,941 --> 00:31:23,541
Even though you wanted someone
to listen to you.

504
00:31:23,541 --> 00:31:25,541
Yes, I wanted that.

505
00:31:25,541 --> 00:31:29,273
Well, the adults were all nice people.

506
00:31:29,273 --> 00:31:32,469
But they couldn't always focus on me alone.

507
00:31:32,469 --> 00:31:34,779
Sometimes I'd catch the guy
from the bookstore

508
00:31:34,779 --> 00:31:36,485
who came to donate picture books,

509
00:31:36,485 --> 00:31:38,413
and get him to listen to me.

510
00:31:38,413 --> 00:31:41,280
Gosh, that must have been a nuisance for him.

511
00:31:45,769 --> 00:31:47,497
What about you?

512
00:31:48,697 --> 00:31:50,429
Huh?

513
00:31:51,929 --> 00:31:55,385
Tell me your life story.

514
00:31:55,385 --> 00:31:58,246
I like listening to other people
talk about their lives

515
00:31:58,246 --> 00:31:59,863
as much as I do talking.

516
00:32:02,397 --> 00:32:05,697
All right, then until the drying is done.

517
00:32:06,582 --> 00:32:07,619
Okay.

518
00:32:07,619 --> 00:32:09,769
I don't know if it's
because of my upbringing,

519
00:32:09,769 --> 00:32:11,557
but I suddenly get the urge

520
00:32:11,557 --> 00:32:14,322
to just run away from wherever I am
over and over.

521
00:32:15,017 --> 00:32:16,477
A disappearing habit, I guess.

522
00:32:16,477 --> 00:32:20,229
Ah, that's the most troublesome of habits.

523
00:32:20,229 --> 00:32:24,037
<i>I poured out things
I'd never told anyone before.</i>

524
00:32:24,037 --> 00:32:27,945
<i>About my parents, the urge to run away,</i>

525
00:32:27,945 --> 00:32:29,933
<i>always playing the nice guy,</i>

526
00:32:29,933 --> 00:32:34,247
<i>building temporary relationships,
then running and cutting ties,</i>

527
00:32:34,247 --> 00:32:38,822
<i>then connecting again
and running away again, over and over.</i>

528
00:32:47,393 --> 00:32:48,717
What?

529
00:32:48,717 --> 00:32:51,369
Are you really going to stop talking
just because your clothes are done drying?

530
00:32:51,369 --> 00:32:52,749
At this point in your story?

531
00:32:52,749 --> 00:32:54,721
Can I keep talking?

532
00:32:54,721 --> 00:32:56,993
Sure, go ahead.

533
00:32:56,993 --> 00:32:58,748
Besides, mine's not done yet.

534
00:33:01,109 --> 00:33:03,825
Our first date was a movie.

535
00:33:03,825 --> 00:33:05,645
Oh, nice. Classic.

536
00:33:05,645 --> 00:33:09,340
I liked movies
and often rented them to watch.

537
00:33:09,340 --> 00:33:13,589
But the movie theater was tough.

538
00:33:13,589 --> 00:33:17,613
<i>We kept talking while waiting for
Mrs. Sonoda's laundry to finish drying.</i>

539
00:33:17,613 --> 00:33:21,725
She'd throw in comments,
act overly surprised,

540
00:33:21,725 --> 00:33:24,605
and laugh while listening.

541
00:33:24,605 --> 00:33:28,633
But she never said things like
"That's impossible" or "That's not good."

542
00:33:28,633 --> 00:33:31,709
She never once said anything like that.

543
00:33:34,509 --> 00:33:36,290
It's finished. Good timing.

544
00:33:40,605 --> 00:33:42,661
So that was about ten years ago?

545
00:33:42,661 --> 00:33:43,973
Yes.

546
00:33:43,973 --> 00:33:47,045
So you're cured now?
Of the disappearing habit, I mean?

547
00:33:47,045 --> 00:33:50,257
Well, because Sacchan has been here
these last ten years.

548
00:33:50,257 --> 00:33:53,361
Another lovey-dovey story.

549
00:33:53,361 --> 00:33:57,082
But it doesn't feel like it's cured.

550
00:33:59,221 --> 00:34:02,549
So you still want to run away, huh?

551
00:34:02,549 --> 00:34:04,433
Yes.

552
00:34:08,797 --> 00:34:10,893
The towels are fluffy.

553
00:34:10,893 --> 00:34:12,713
Itsuki will be happy.

554
00:34:12,713 --> 00:34:16,082
He likes thick, fluffy, soft towels.

555
00:34:16,917 --> 00:34:18,790
- Is that so?
- Yes.

556
00:34:22,385 --> 00:34:24,509
Our place is right here.

557
00:34:24,509 --> 00:34:27,117
Oh nice, a detached house.

558
00:34:27,117 --> 00:34:30,049
Sorry for talking so much.

559
00:34:30,049 --> 00:34:31,125
No problem.

560
00:34:31,125 --> 00:34:32,953
Well, then.

561
00:34:32,953 --> 00:34:35,040
When will you next be
going to the laundromat?

562
00:34:36,425 --> 00:34:40,332
When will you listen to my life story?

563
00:34:43,753 --> 00:34:47,413
How about the third Friday of next month?

564
00:34:48,361 --> 00:34:50,153
Got it.

565
00:34:56,269 --> 00:34:58,497
<i>And so from then on,
on the third Friday of every month,</i>

566
00:34:58,497 --> 00:35:03,393
I talked with Mrs. Sonoda
until the washing and drying was done.

567
00:35:04,613 --> 00:35:07,245
<i>We had silly small talk</i>

568
00:35:07,245 --> 00:35:12,748
<i>and shared traumas from our childhoods
that we'd never told anyone before.</i>

569
00:35:13,513 --> 00:35:15,433
The reason I could talk to
Mrs. Sonoda about anything

570
00:35:15,433 --> 00:35:19,889
was because she was a stranger
unrelated to my life,

571
00:35:19,889 --> 00:35:21,957
and it didn't matter if she hated me.

572
00:35:31,065 --> 00:35:35,832
- Sho finally fell asleep.
- Thanks.

573
00:35:40,405 --> 00:35:42,757
- Hey, Itsuki.
- Hmm?

574
00:35:42,757 --> 00:35:45,641
I'm super fast at replying to
messages on LINE, right?

575
00:35:45,641 --> 00:35:48,373
Yeah, you read messages instantly.

576
00:35:48,373 --> 00:35:51,481
Exactly. So if someone like me

577
00:35:51,481 --> 00:35:54,209
suddenly went off the grid,
what would you think?

578
00:35:54,209 --> 00:35:56,365
I'd be worried, of course.

579
00:35:56,365 --> 00:35:58,393
I'd think that you'd collapsed
somewhere or something.

580
00:35:58,393 --> 00:36:02,707
Or... well, that you'd lost
or broken your phone.

581
00:36:03,417 --> 00:36:05,957
- Right?
- Huh?

582
00:36:06,573 --> 00:36:10,313
Hey, listen to this.
Moka's mom invited me to afternoon tea.

583
00:36:10,313 --> 00:36:13,169
- It's 15,000 yen per person.
- 15,000 yen?

584
00:36:13,169 --> 00:36:15,129
- I blurted out "That's expensive!"
- Of course you would.

585
00:36:15,129 --> 00:36:19,981
<i>I had a baseless confidence
that I might be the only one</i>

586
00:36:19,981 --> 00:36:22,540
<i>who could understand Mr. Seo.</i>

587
00:36:27,089 --> 00:36:30,649
Tomorrow's the third Friday of the month,
so it's the deadline.

588
00:36:30,649 --> 00:36:33,205
I want to be home by ten.

589
00:36:33,205 --> 00:36:35,189
I'll make dinner. What do you want?

590
00:36:35,189 --> 00:36:36,405
Thanks.

591
00:36:36,405 --> 00:36:39,529
Oh, but you can just
order delivery or something.

592
00:36:39,529 --> 00:36:41,625
When you're alone, do whatever you want.

593
00:36:41,625 --> 00:36:43,249
It's fine.

594
00:36:43,249 --> 00:36:47,581
Hey, on Fridays, don't you ever
go out anywhere alone?

595
00:36:47,581 --> 00:36:50,445
I do go out. Like to the laundromat,
for example.

596
00:36:50,445 --> 00:36:53,113
That's just chores. Do something fun.

597
00:36:53,113 --> 00:36:55,565
I like doing it. Want a beer?

598
00:36:55,565 --> 00:36:57,949
Yeah.

599
00:36:57,949 --> 00:36:59,933
Thanks.

600
00:36:59,933 --> 00:37:01,521
- Here.
- Cheers.

601
00:37:01,521 --> 00:37:04,741
<i>As long as I had that time once a month,</i>

602
00:37:04,741 --> 00:37:07,029
<i>I wouldn't run away from here.</i>

603
00:37:07,029 --> 00:37:10,609
<i>I wouldn't feel anxious about
the happiness right in front of me.</i>

604
00:37:10,609 --> 00:37:12,609
<i>It was a time</i>

605
00:37:12,609 --> 00:37:16,629
<i>for me to distance myself from the people
I'd be with for the rest of my life,</i>

606
00:37:16,629 --> 00:37:17,845
<i>and at the same time,</i>

607
00:37:17,845 --> 00:37:21,873
<i>a time to reaffirm my happiness
of being with them.</i>

608
00:37:22,485 --> 00:37:25,029
<i>Even though I wanted to be
a little bit apart from them,</i>

609
00:37:25,029 --> 00:37:29,005
<i>we mostly still just talked about
the people we lived with.</i>

610
00:37:29,005 --> 00:37:30,573
<i>For me,</i>

611
00:37:30,573 --> 00:37:35,045
<i>he was just someone
who listened to me once a month.</i>

612
00:37:35,045 --> 00:37:37,013
<i>And that was nice.</i>

613
00:37:37,729 --> 00:37:40,765
<i>I'm not doing anything wrong,</i>

614
00:37:40,765 --> 00:37:44,248
<i>but it's still hard to tell Itsuki
about Mr. Seo.</i>

615
00:37:52,837 --> 00:37:59,169
[I'm not confident that Itsuki would
understand my relationship with Mr. Seo.]

616
00:38:13,590 --> 00:38:17,365
You could call it friends or neighbors,

617
00:38:17,365 --> 00:38:20,373
maybe there's a name for it,

618
00:38:21,265 --> 00:38:24,998
but my relationship
with Mrs. Sonoda was like that.

619
00:38:27,389 --> 00:38:30,941
With you, we decided that we didn't
have to talk about things we didn't want to,

620
00:38:30,941 --> 00:38:32,465
and that was comfortable.

621
00:38:32,465 --> 00:38:36,097
But with Mrs. Sonoda, I could say anything,

622
00:38:36,097 --> 00:38:38,253
and it didn't matter if she hated me.

623
00:38:38,253 --> 00:38:40,201
Yeah, okay, I get it.

624
00:38:41,401 --> 00:38:45,269
I kept quiet because I wasn't confident
that you'd understand.

625
00:38:45,269 --> 00:38:48,469
I thought that no matter how
I explained it, it would upset you,

626
00:38:48,469 --> 00:38:50,623
and that you'd suspect me.

627
00:38:52,237 --> 00:38:56,561
Someone whom you don't care
if they hate you. I see.

628
00:38:56,561 --> 00:39:01,101
And I'm someone whom
you don't want to hate you.

629
00:39:01,101 --> 00:39:03,101
Yeah.

630
00:39:03,101 --> 00:39:05,025
Oh.

631
00:39:05,025 --> 00:39:07,801
Like something you don't want to get dirty,
even though it's your favorite outfit.

632
00:39:07,801 --> 00:39:10,165
And so you never wear it, right?

633
00:39:12,949 --> 00:39:15,533
While something that
you splatter meat sauce all over,

634
00:39:15,533 --> 00:39:17,693
eating pasta while wearing,
and saying "This is delicious"

635
00:39:17,693 --> 00:39:19,832
feels more like family.

636
00:39:20,765 --> 00:39:22,686
If it gets dirty, just wash it.

637
00:39:23,327 --> 00:39:26,041
I'm not good at doing laundry,

638
00:39:26,041 --> 00:39:27,957
but I am pretty good at getting stains out.

639
00:39:29,665 --> 00:39:31,473
I know.

640
00:39:32,157 --> 00:39:34,207
You know that I'm good at getting stains out?

641
00:39:35,289 --> 00:39:39,123
I know that I was
getting it wrong little by little.

642
00:39:43,649 --> 00:39:47,481
I'd throw things away before they got dirty
and then buy new ones.

643
00:39:47,481 --> 00:39:49,623
That's how I've lived my life.

644
00:39:51,117 --> 00:39:54,873
But when I didn't want to get it dirty
and also didn't want to throw it away,

645
00:39:57,829 --> 00:40:00,207
I didn't know what to do.

646
00:40:06,509 --> 00:40:09,248
- Wait.
- Wait?

647
00:40:09,917 --> 00:40:12,901
That's not fair! You're the one who...

648
00:40:12,901 --> 00:40:16,457
I have times when I want to
get away from here too, you know.

649
00:40:16,457 --> 00:40:19,290
Maybe for just a few seconds, maybe a year.

650
00:40:23,913 --> 00:40:27,457
She wasn't someone
completely unrelated to your life.

651
00:40:28,293 --> 00:40:31,040
You took her to meet your parents.

652
00:40:33,007 --> 00:40:36,941
You're leaving now? Where are you going?

653
00:40:39,157 --> 00:40:41,707
To Mrs. Sonoda's husband's place?

654
00:40:45,045 --> 00:40:46,797
So what if I am?

655
00:40:49,793 --> 00:40:51,915
Can you say "Don't go"?

656
00:41:29,845 --> 00:41:31,605
Your name...

657
00:41:34,509 --> 00:41:36,189
Okay you two, your luggage first...

658
00:41:36,189 --> 00:41:39,333
[3-4-4 Minamisensu, Minami-Shinshu,
Nagano Prefecture]

659
00:41:43,817 --> 00:41:45,501
Good morning.

660
00:41:45,501 --> 00:41:47,298
- Huh?
- Nice weather we're having here today.

661
00:41:47,298 --> 00:41:49,257
A little too hot, though.

662
00:41:49,257 --> 00:41:50,477
What are you doing here?

663
00:41:50,477 --> 00:41:52,277
Today's the day Miki is coming over.

664
00:41:52,277 --> 00:41:54,265
Oh, she's my sister-in-law.
My husband's sister.

665
00:41:54,265 --> 00:41:55,715
She's going to watch Sho for me.

666
00:41:55,715 --> 00:41:57,457
That's not what I meant.

667
00:41:58,401 --> 00:42:01,040
I might actually want to tag along.

668
00:42:02,141 --> 00:42:04,269
Aren't you feeling a little uneasy?

669
00:42:04,269 --> 00:42:06,873
But this isn't any of your business,
Mrs. Sonoda.

670
00:42:07,625 --> 00:42:09,208
If it's not any of my business,

671
00:42:09,208 --> 00:42:11,733
then what's the harm
in you inconveniencing me a little?

672
00:42:11,733 --> 00:42:16,289
As I was hearing about
your history of disappearing, Mr. Seo,

673
00:42:16,289 --> 00:42:18,037
I found myself getting
more and more intrigued.

674
00:42:18,037 --> 00:42:20,023
No way. Disappearing is a bad idea.
Absolutely not.

675
00:42:20,023 --> 00:42:21,241
- It'd cause trouble for your family.
- Oh, no, no.

676
00:42:21,241 --> 00:42:23,113
I wouldn't do a real disappearing act.

677
00:42:23,113 --> 00:42:26,953
I have a husband and a child,
and a home to return to. That's my happiness.

678
00:42:26,953 --> 00:42:28,589
Then that's even more reason.

679
00:42:28,589 --> 00:42:32,123
It sounds like
you haven't exactly kicked the habit.

680
00:42:32,769 --> 00:42:34,132
Right.

681
00:42:34,132 --> 00:42:38,285
So I figured that maybe a little
pressure release would do you good.

682
00:42:38,285 --> 00:42:41,321
- A one-day disappearance, just for today?
- Exactly.

683
00:42:41,321 --> 00:42:44,007
If I get home before
anyone realizes I'm gone,

684
00:42:44,007 --> 00:42:46,805
then it technically never happened.

685
00:42:46,805 --> 00:42:49,365
And since we already chat once a month,

686
00:42:49,365 --> 00:42:52,290
how about a mini-disappearance
every six months or so?

687
00:42:52,973 --> 00:42:54,321
I like it.

688
00:42:54,321 --> 00:42:57,233
Although some people
might call that having an affair.

689
00:42:57,233 --> 00:43:00,373
Ah, yeah.

690
00:43:01,705 --> 00:43:03,085
Well, isn't that okay?

691
00:43:03,085 --> 00:43:07,741
A little bit of guilt like this is worth it,
even if we only talk once a month.

692
00:43:07,741 --> 00:43:09,015
And even just that,

693
00:43:09,015 --> 00:43:11,505
some people would still make a fuss
and call it an emotional affair.

694
00:43:11,505 --> 00:43:13,305
If they'll never understand us anyway,

695
00:43:13,305 --> 00:43:15,873
then let's just do something
a little scandalous.

696
00:43:18,417 --> 00:43:20,749
You're right.

697
00:43:20,749 --> 00:43:22,882
We will be departing shortly.

698
00:43:22,882 --> 00:43:25,290
- Please hurry.
- It's about to leave.

699
00:43:29,737 --> 00:43:32,665
<i>A one-day disappearance,
one that no one would ever know about.</i>

700
00:43:33,845 --> 00:43:38,582
<i>I'd planned to be home by nightfall,
where Sacchan would be waiting for me.</i>

701
00:43:45,048 --> 00:43:53,897
[June 21, 2025]
